A||N||7||fierose||Please Help!!  Fuel Pump Trouble||||1||
Z||000000||fierose||09-17-2000||07:34 PM||||Just the other day I went to start my car at the mall and it took a good minute or two for it to start and when I drove it home went to start it again and nothing. I'm not getting any fuel to my fuel filter.  Just today I disconnected the fuel pump/sending unit cable and hooked it up directly to my battery to see if i could bypass everything and run the pump.  My question is does the fuel pump make a noticable sound when running, even if it might not be pumping gas?  And also their are 3 wires on connector i'm assuming it's the red and black but their is a pinkish wire with a white stripe also.  It is possible I could be low on gas and with the car jacked up it might not be reaching the pump in the tank.  I did put a gallon in to be sure though.  Just wanted to make absolutely sure the pump was bad, just dropped the tank a couple months ago to fix some rust damage and it's not fun.  Any help would be greatly appreciated I'm a student and start a new job on Wed.  Thanks ahead time<P>Josh D<BR>85 2.5L<BR>238,000||||reg||1
Z||000001||terryk||09-17-2000||07:42 PM||||The pumps do have a mechanical fuse in them that breaks incase the impeller stalls. If it breaks, the pump runs and nothing comes out.<P>There are other possible causes but all are in the tank so you'll know when you get there.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierose||09-17-2000||07:50 PM||||Do you know if the fuel pumps make an audible sound when running.  I searched the archive and found out which of the wire to hotwire.  My guess is maybe the fuel isn't reaching the pump, there's a blockage in the line or bad pump.  Just wanted to be absolutely sure.<P>Thanks again<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||09-17-2000||08:43 PM||||I had an 84 2.5 (obviously) that would start for 30 seconds and then cut out. I would get a lean O2 code. I had flow to the fuel filter, but not great pressure. To give you the Cliff-notes version, I would say that it turned out to be the 3" long rubber hose from the fuel pump, to the steel line in side the tank. This line had a longitudunal split in it that allowed the pressure to bleed off before leaving the tank. I agree, pull the tank and figure it out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fearless Freep||09-17-2000||08:51 PM||||I can hear my fuel pump when you first turn the ignition to get ready to start. And I hear the fuel pump for about 3 seconds after turning the car off.  Kinda sounds like a small electric pencil sharpener thats stuck under a pillow.  Hope that helps...||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||09-17-2000||11:53 PM||||The pumps do have a mechanical fuse in them that breaks incase the impeller stalls. If it breaks, the pump runs and nothing comes out.<P>There are other possible causes but all are in the tank so you'll know when you get there.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000006||Philphine||09-18-2000||12:24 AM||||you should hear the pump for a few seconds when you turn the key to on. you can also disconnect it from the relay and listen while you wire it strait from the battery. you can get a vaccume/pressure tester from auto zone to see if the pressure is up to specs.<P>where in ind. is angola?||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierose||09-18-2000||09:49 AM||||Hey thanks for all the help.  I'm not getting a single sound when I hotwire the pump.  So looks like tomorrow is when I drop the tank.  I was suprised autozone has got the pumps for $36.  They're not AC Delco but they got a lifetime warranty.  By the way Angola is in the northeast corner of the state about 5 minutes from Mich. 5 minutes from Ohio.  But I live in West Lafayette where I got to school at Purdue.  Thanks again.<P>Josh D.||||reg||6
